## PHASE 0: SOURCE PLAN AND RETRIEVAL

1. Inventory every document in the folder: type, period covered, language, date. Flag gaps against the list below.
2. Check the regulator-equivalent filings first. Go to EDINET and TDnet for the latest annual securities report, interim report, quarterly results, earnings release, and any forecast-revision or timely-disclosure notices. If a portal cannot be accessed or searched, go to the company IR site (Japanese pages first, English second) and retrieve the same documents there. State which route worked for each document.
3. Read in the native language (Japanese). Do not rely on English translations when the Japanese original is available; where the English version differs, the Japanese prevails and the difference should be noted. Convert newly retrieved PDFs to markdown with the pdf-to-markdown skill so they can be read in full and cited by page.
4. Source priority:
   a. Mid-term plan, investor day, or capital markets day material (2025 onward)
   b. Latest annual report, latest quarterly filing, results presentation
   c. Management commentary, press releases, TDnet notices, analyst Q&A or briefing summaries
   d. Trade and industry press, used only for market-level context, never for company-specific claims
5. Recency rule: 2025 onward management commentary beats older filings. If they contradict, say so explicitly.
6. Analyst Q&A: if a transcript or Q&A summary exists, retrieve it. If none exists, state "no Q&A material available." Do not invent one.
7. Deliver: a short table of documents obtained (document, period, source route, language, pages) and a list of documents that could not be obtained.

