## STYLE RULES

- Body text in English. Spanish appears only as inline glosses for technical or chemical terms the first time they appear, e.g. "colloidal silica (sílice coloidal, abrasive particles suspended in liquid)", plus the Spanish line under section 1. No Spanish section summaries.
- Define every industry term inline the first time. Never assume the reader knows it. Give the original Japanese term in parentheses when the translation could be ambiguous.
- Insight before data: lead with the implication, then the number. Never open a sentence with a raw figure.
- Every figure carries its fiscal year in company convention (FY03/2026). Never "recently."
- No em dashes. No standalone "likely"; use "consistent with" or "pointing to." No arrows between data points.
- No ratings, price targets, or buy/sell opinions.
- Depth over brevity. Use tables where they carry the information better than prose.
- Yen with unit stated; give USD only with the rate used.
